{id:'3.1',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Panhypoproteinemia and abdominal effusion',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 3.1 · CP3 slides 13, 48, 51',signal:'4-year-old castrated male Yorkshire Terrier',history:'Six weeks of diarrhea, weight loss and abdominal enlargement. No skin disease, medications or recent IV fluids.',exam:'BCS 3/5, mild ascites, normal mucous membranes, temperature 38.6 °C.',panels:[{name:'CBC and protein pattern',rows:[['HCT','42 %','37–55'],['Total protein','3.7 g/dL','5.4–7.5'],['Albumin','1.4 g/dL','2.5–3.8'],['Globulin','2.3 g/dL','2.5–4.5'],['Lymphocytes','0.7 ×10³/µL','1.0–4.8']]},{name:'Chemistry and UPC',rows:[['Cholesterol','88 mg/dL','110–320'],['Total calcium','7.6 mg/dL','9.7–11.5'],['BUN','9 mg/dL','7–27'],['Urine protein:creatinine','0.2','<0.5']]}],smear:'Not applicable; chemistry pattern case.',photos:['HCT and total plasma protein interpretation table (CP3 slide 13)','Ascites image (CP3 slide 49)'],questions:[{q:'Which protein pattern is present?',options:['Panhypoproteinemia, with both albumin and globulin decreased','Selective hyperglobulinemia','Normal protein status because HCT is normal','Hemoconcentration from dehydration'],answer:0,why:'Total protein 3.7, albumin 1.4 and globulin 2.3 g/dL are all below their listed reference intervals. The HCT is normal, which argues against simple simultaneous RBC and protein loss.'},{q:'Which findings favor intestinal protein loss over protein-losing nephropathy?',options:['Chronic diarrhea, low albumin and globulin, low cholesterol and normal UPC','Marked proteinuria and isolated low albumin','Raised albumin and globulin from dehydration','Isolated high HCT'],answer:0,why:'With UPC 0.2, substantial renal protein loss is less supported. Concurrent albumin/globulin decline, chronic diarrhea, low cholesterol and lymphopenia fit an enteric loss pattern.'}],differentials:[{name:'Protein-losing enteropathy (PLE)',tier:'best',support:'Diarrhea, weight loss, ascites, panhypoproteinemia, low cholesterol, normal UPC',against:'Underlying intestinal lesion not identified',test:'Fecal evaluation, abdominal ultrasound and indicated GI biopsy'},{name:'Protein-losing glomerulopathy (PLN)',tier:'possible',support:'Can cause hypoalbuminemia',against:'UPC 0.2 and low globulin make major renal protein loss less likely',test:'Repeat quantitative urine protein assessment if clinical concern persists'},{name:'Hepatic synthetic failure',tier:'possible',support:'Reduced synthetic function can lower proteins',against:'No other evidence of hepatic failure supplied',test:'Bile acids and liver function assessment if indicated'},{name:'Hemorrhage or dilution',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can lower circulating protein',against:'HCT normal and no hemorrhage or recent IV fluids',test:'Review hydration and bleeding history'}]},
{id:'3.2',kind:'SBA',title:'Dog · Hemoconcentration with lipemic refractometer error',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 3.2 · CP3 slides 12–15, 50, 73',signal:'9-year-old spayed female Miniature Schnauzer',history:'PU/PD for weeks, two days of vomiting, anorexia.',exam:'Approximately 8% dehydrated, skin tent, tacky mucous membranes; plasma milky and slightly pink.',panels:[{name:'HCT and protein',rows:[['HCT','58 %','37–55'],['Refractometer TPP','9.8 g/dL','5.4–7.5'],['Biochemical total protein','9.0 g/dL','5.4–7.5'],['Albumin','4.4 g/dL','2.5–3.8'],['Globulin','4.6 g/dL','2.5–4.5']]},{name:'Chemistry',rows:[['Glucose','540 mg/dL','65–120'],['Triglycerides','1150 mg/dL','<150'],['BUN','58 mg/dL','7–27'],['Creatinine','1.9 mg/dL','0.5–1.4']]}],smear:'Not applicable; chemistry and fluid-balance case.',photos:['Hematocrit tube with lipemic plasma (CP3 slide 8)','Before/after heating hematocrit tubes for fibrinogen (CP3 slide 37)'],questions:[{q:'How do the HCT, albumin and globulin change together?',options:['Concurrent increases support dehydration-associated hemoconcentration','Isolated monoclonal globulin production is proven','Severe red cell loss with normal fluid balance','A normal protein pattern'],answer:0,why:'HCT, albumin and globulin are all high in a clinically dehydrated patient. This supports hemoconcentration rather than selective hyperglobulinemia.'},{q:'Which interpretation best explains the HCT, protein and refractometer results? (Original SBA)',options:['Chronic antigenic stimulation with true hyperglobulinemia; refractometer is exact','Dehydration (hemoconcentration) with falsely high refractometric TPP due to lipemia, glucose and urea nitrogen','Absolute erythrocytosis with plasma cell neoplasia','Hemolysis falsely lowered refractometric TPP','Splenic contraction from excitement'],answer:1,why:'The measured refractometric TPP (9.8) exceeds biochemical total protein (9.0). Lipemia and high nonprotein solutes interfere with refractometry, while elevations in HCT, albumin and globulin indicate hemoconcentration.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'3.3',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Broad-based gammopathy after tick exposure',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 3.3 · CP3 slides 13, 26, 31, 56–63',signal:'6-year-old castrated male German Shepherd',history:'Outdoor kennel dog in endemic region; two months of weight loss and lethargy, tick exposure, intermittent fever.',exam:'Temperature 39.8 °C, pale gums, small gingival petechiae and mild generalized lymphadenopathy.',panels:[{name:'CBC and protein',rows:[['HCT','27 %','37–55'],['Platelets','48 ×10³/µL','200–500'],['Total protein','9.1 g/dL','5.4–7.5'],['Albumin','2.2 g/dL','2.5–3.8'],['Globulin','6.9 g/dL','2.5–4.5']]},{name:'Chemistry',rows:[['Total calcium','10.1 mg/dL','9.7–11.5'],['Creatinine','0.9 mg/dL','0.5–1.4']]}],smear:'Serum protein electrophoresis tracing has a broad gamma peak substantially wider than albumin.',photos:['Broad polyclonal gamma band with chronic E. canis (CP3 slide 63)','Polyclonal versus monoclonal SPE comparison (CP3 slide 57)'],questions:[{q:'How should the protein electrophoresis peak shape be interpreted?',options:['Broad gamma increase suggests polyclonal antigenic stimulation','A sharp monoclonal protein is proved','No immunoglobulin expansion','Albumin excess only'],answer:0,why:'A broad-based gamma peak suggests products of multiple B-cell clones (polyclonal gammopathy). Compare width to the albumin peak; some polyclonal patterns may mimic monoclonal peaks.'},{q:'What major CBC and protein abnormalities accompany this?',options:['Anemia, marked thrombocytopenia, high globulin and low albumin','Polycythemia, thrombocytosis and normal proteins','Normal CBC with selective hyperalbuminemia','Only decreased creatinine'],answer:0,why:'HCT 27% and platelets 48 ×10³/µL are low, globulin 6.9 g/dL is high, and albumin 2.2 g/dL is low. A chronic inflammatory/infectious process may account for this combination.'}],differentials:[{name:'Chronic Ehrlichia canis infection',tier:'best',support:'Tick exposure, anemia, thrombocytopenia, low albumin and broad polyclonal gamma pattern',against:'Needs organism-specific confirmation',test:'E. canis PCR or serology interpreted with timing and exposure'},{name:'Other chronic infection / antigenic stimulation',tier:'possible',support:'Broad polyclonal hyperglobulinemia and chronic illness',against:'No other pathogen or focus identified',test:'Directed infectious disease testing'},{name:'Immune-mediated disease',tier:'possible',support:'Chronic antigenic activation may produce polyclonal proteins',against:'Tick-associated infection has more specific evidence',test:'Targeted immune testing if indicated after infectious evaluation'},{name:'Immune stimulation secondary to neoplasm',tier:'possible',support:'Some malignancies stimulate polyclonal immunoglobulins',against:'No neoplasm demonstrated',test:'Examination and targeted imaging or sampling'},{name:'Plasma cell neoplasm / lymphoma',tier:'unlikely',support:'May produce marked hyperglobulinemia',against:'The gamma band is broad, not a typical sharp monoclonal spike',test:'Repeat SPE and pursue directed work-up if suspicion remains'}]},
{id:'3.4',kind:'SBA',title:'Cat · Narrow monoclonal gamma peak',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 3.4 · CP3 slides 25, 57–62',signal:'13-year-old spayed female domestic shorthair',history:'Weight loss, PU/PD, intermittent epistaxis.',exam:'Thin with pale gums, mild retinal vessel tortuosity; no palpable masses.',panels:[{name:'CBC and serum proteins',rows:[['HCT','23 %','30–45'],['Total protein','10.6 g/dL','6.0–8.0'],['Albumin','3.0 g/dL','2.4–3.8'],['Globulin','7.6 g/dL','2.8–5.1']]},{name:'Chemistry',rows:[['BUN','28 mg/dL','16–36']]}],smear:'SPE: tall, narrow gamma peak about as wide as albumin. Marrow aspirate: increased atypical plasma cells.',photos:['Monoclonal feline SPE trace (CP3 slide 61)','Neoplastic plasma cells in marrow aspirate (CP3 slide 62)'],questions:[{q:'What is the key serum protein pattern?',options:['Marked hyperglobulinemia with normal albumin and narrow gamma spike','Concurrent decline in albumin and globulin','Pure hyperalbuminemia from dehydration','Normal gamma protein concentrations'],answer:0,why:'Total protein 10.6 and globulin 7.6 g/dL are markedly elevated. Albumin is within range, and the narrow sharp gamma band raises concern for a clonal immunoglobulin.'},{q:'Which finding most strongly supports a monoclonal rather than polyclonal gammopathy? (Original SBA)',options:['Globulin 7.6 g/dL by itself','Narrow sharp gamma peak about the width of albumin','Total protein over 10 g/dL','Broad-based gamma peak','Low HCT with high TPP'],answer:1,why:'A monoclonal immunoglobulin migrates as a relatively narrow peak because it derives from a single clone. A broad band suggests polyclonal antigenic stimulation. Marrow atypical plasma cells provide additional case context.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'3.5',kind:'DDx',title:'Foal · Failure of passive transfer and septic arthritis',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 3.5 · CP3 slides 6, 28, 30–31, 35, 37–39, 51–54',signal:'3-day-old Thoroughbred foal',history:'Born overnight; nursing was not observed to be adequate. Dam did not run milk before foaling. Now depressed with swollen hot left carpus.',exam:'Temperature 39.6 °C, HR 120/min, weak suckle, left carpal effusion, no diarrhea or IV fluids.',panels:[{name:'Protein and hematocrit',rows:[['HCT','33 %','32–46'],['Refractometer plasma TPP','4.8 g/dL','4–6 (neonate)'],['Serum total protein','4.2 g/dL','4–6 (neonate)'],['Albumin','2.3 g/dL','2.6–3.4'],['Globulin','1.9 g/dL','2.5–4.0'],['Serum IgG','320 mg/dL','>800']]},{name:'Inflammatory markers',rows:[['Fibrinogen (heat estimate)','600 mg/dL','100–400'],['SAA','520 mg/L','<20']]}],smear:'Fibrinogen by heat precipitation = plasma TP before heating (4.8) minus after heating (4.2) = 0.6 g/dL = 600 mg/dL.',photos:['Foal SPE with low gamma region (CP3 slide 54)','Hematocrit tubes before and after heating (CP3 slides 37–38)','SAA and fibrinogen time-response graph (CP3 slide 30)'],questions:[{q:'What does the serum IgG of 320 mg/dL suggest in this three-day-old foal?',options:['Failure of passive transfer despite neonatal-range total protein','Confirmed normal colostral immunoglobulin transfer','Pure adult-type dehydration','Proof of a normal immune response'],answer:0,why:'IgG 320 mg/dL is below the supplied >800 mg/dL target; the lecture case interprets this as failure of passive transfer. Total protein can appear normal for a neonate despite a major IgG deficit.'},{q:'What is the heat-precipitation fibrinogen estimate when plasma TPP is 4.8 and post-heat TPP is 4.2 g/dL?',options:['600 mg/dL','60 mg/dL','6 mg/dL','6000 mg/dL'],answer:0,why:'Difference = 0.6 g/dL = 600 mg/dL. The estimate is increased; marked SAA elevation and a hot joint further support an active inflammatory response.'}],differentials:[{name:'Failure of passive transfer',tier:'best',support:'Inadequate observed nursing, low IgG and low globulin in a neonate',against:'Coexisting sepsis requires separate evaluation',test:'Repeat/confirm IgG assessment and evaluate immune support per neonatal care protocol'},{name:'Neonatal sepsis with septic arthritis',tier:'possible',support:'Fever, weak suckle, hot swollen joint, SAA 520 and fibrinogen 600',against:'Underlying organism not established',test:'Joint sampling/culture and systemic sepsis evaluation as clinically appropriate'},{name:'Overhydration',tier:'unlikely',support:'Could dilute immunoglobulins',against:'No IV fluids and strong inflammatory marker increases',test:'Hydration history and serial measurements'},{name:'Hemorrhage',tier:'unlikely',support:'Could lower circulating proteins',against:'HCT normal and fibrinogen increased',test:'Review bleeding evidence'},{name:'Protein-losing enteropathy',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can decrease protein concentrations',against:'No diarrhea and inflammatory markers elevated',test:'GI evaluation only if subsequently indicated'}]}
,
{id:'4.1',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Persistent neutropenia and granulocytic hypoplasia',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 4.1 · CP4 Reasons for Bone Marrow Examinations; Interpretation of M:E Ratio',signal:'5-year-old spayed female Labrador',history:'Four days of lethargy and fever; persistent neutropenia for three weeks on repeat CBCs. No new medication except a flea product.',exam:'Temperature 39.9 °C; pink mucous membranes; no lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly.',panels:[{name:'Peripheral CBC',rows:[['HCT','44 %','37–55'],['WBC','2.9 ×10³/µL','6.0–17.0'],['Segmented neutrophils','0.3 ×10³/µL','3.0–11.5'],['Band neutrophils','0 ×10³/µL','0–0.3'],['Lymphocytes','2.1 ×10³/µL','1.0–4.8'],['Platelets','290 ×10³/µL','200–500']]},{name:'Bone marrow',rows:[['Marrow cellularity','Normal','Qualitative'],['M:E ratio','0.40','0.75–2.53'],['Megakaryocytes / low-power field','8','3–15']]}],smear:'Marrow aspirate: normal cellularity, few granulocytic precursors; erythroid series and megakaryocytes normal; no blasts.',photos:['Granulocytic hypoplasia in a neutropenic dog (CP4 slide titled Granulocytic Hypoplasia)'],questions:[{q:'Which peripheral blood-cell line is selectively reduced?',options:['Neutrophils, with normal HCT and platelets','Erythrocytes alone','Platelets alone','All three cell lines'],answer:0,why:'Segmented neutrophils 0.3 ×10³/µL are markedly reduced, whereas HCT 44% and platelets 290 ×10³/µL fall within the supplied intervals. The pattern is isolated severe neutropenia.'},{q:'How should a low M:E ratio of 0.40 be interpreted with a normal HCT and few granulocyte precursors?',options:['Granulocytic hypoplasia lowering the myeloid side of the ratio','Erythroid hyperplasia must always be present','Normal marrow because the ratio is within range','Megakaryocyte overproduction is the primary explanation'],answer:0,why:'M:E compares myeloid and erythroid precursors. With normal HCT and erythroid maturation, few granulocytic precursors lower the ratio; marrow cellularity must be considered before attributing a ratio to one lineage.'}],differentials:[{name:'Drug- or toxin-associated granulocytic hypoplasia',tier:'best',support:'Persistent isolated neutropenia, low marrow M:E and few granulocyte precursors',against:'Specific causative exposure not established',test:'Detailed drug and exposure review; serial CBC and medication history'},{name:'Infectious marrow suppression',tier:'possible',support:'Febrile neutropenic patient; infection can affect granulopoiesis',against:'Organism and exposure not identified',test:'Directed infectious testing guided by region, history and examination'},{name:'Immune-mediated precursor destruction',tier:'possible',support:'Selective neutrophil precursor depletion is possible',against:'Exclude drugs and infection before attributing cause to immune mechanisms',test:'Specialist hematology work-up if persistent'},{name:'Maturation-arrest neutropenia',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can cause neutropenia',against:'Very few precursors are observed, not accumulated immature stages',test:'Review marrow stage distribution'},{name:'Marrow neoplasia or myelofibrosis',tier:'unlikely',support:'May cause cytopenias',against:'No blasts, normal cellularity and other lines preserved',test:'Core review if clinical suspicion persists'}]},
{id:'4.2',kind:'SBA',title:'Horse · Marrow erythroid hyperplasia and M:E ratio',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 4.2 · CP4 equine marrow slides, M:E example 0.16',signal:'8-year-old Quarter Horse gelding',history:'Ten days of lethargy, poor exercise tolerance and pale gums after a colic episode; heavy parasite burden suspected.',exam:'HR 56/min, pale mucous membranes, temperature 37.8 °C, no icterus.',panels:[{name:'Blood and marrow',rows:[['HCT','15 %','32–48'],['Neutrophils','5.1 ×10³/µL','2.7–6.7'],['Peripheral blood reticulocytes','None seen','None normally'],['Marrow cellularity','Hypercellular','Qualitative'],['Marrow M:E ratio','0.16','0.50–1.50']]}],smear:'Marrow aspirate and core are hypercellular with marked erythroid hyperplasia; polychromatophilic rubricytes and metarubricytes abundant.',photos:['Equine marrow aspirate/core with erythroid hyperplasia (CP4 slides Equine Bone Marrow; Anemia with Normal Neutrophil Count)'],questions:[{q:'Does absence of circulating reticulocytes exclude marrow regeneration in a horse?',options:['No; horses usually do not release reticulocytes into peripheral blood','Yes; absent reticulocytes establish marrow aplasia','Yes; all mammals have the same circulating reticulocyte response','It confirms neutrophil hypoplasia'],answer:0,why:'Horses normally do not release reticulocytes into peripheral blood. Hypercellular marrow and marked erythroid hyperplasia support erythropoietic activity despite no circulating reticulocytes.'},{q:'What does the M:E ratio of 0.16 most likely indicate in this horse? (Original SBA)',options:['Granulocytic hypoplasia','Erythroid hyperplasia, an appropriate marrow response to the anemia','Marrow aplasia','Myelofibrosis','Normal marrow for a horse'],answer:1,why:'The horse has HCT 15% with normal neutrophils, hypercellular marrow and expanded erythroid precursors. An M:E of 0.16 reflects a pronounced erythroid response rather than loss of granulopoiesis.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'4.3',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Pancytopenia and fibrotic marrow',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 4.3 · CP4 Bone Marrow Core Biopsy; Myelofibrosis in a Dog',signal:'7-year-old castrated male mixed-breed dog',history:'Three weeks of weakness and bruising; treated for immune-mediated hemolytic anemia eight months earlier.',exam:'Pale gums, petechiae and ecchymoses, without lymphadenopathy.',panels:[{name:'CBC',rows:[['HCT','17 %','37–55'],['Absolute reticulocytes','6 ×10³/µL','10–110'],['WBC','2.4 ×10³/µL','6.0–17.0'],['Neutrophils','0.9 ×10³/µL','3.0–11.5'],['Platelets','18 ×10³/µL','200–500']]},{name:'Marrow studies',rows:[['Aspirate','Dry tap; few particles','Qualitative'],['Core biopsy','Hypocellular; fibrous tissue; few megakaryocytes','Qualitative']]}],smear:'Second marrow aspirate nearly acellular. Core H&E shows collagen and fat replacing marrow with few hematopoietic cells.',photos:['Myelofibrosis in a dog, core biopsy (CP4)','Normal canine marrow histopathology (CP4)'],questions:[{q:'What does the peripheral CBC indicate?',options:['Pancytopenia with an inadequate regenerative response','Isolated severe thrombocytosis','Regenerative anemia with neutrophilia','Normal blood-cell production'],answer:0,why:'HCT, reticulocytes, WBC/neutrophils and platelets are reduced, implicating loss of multiple cell lines and impaired marrow production.'},{q:'Why is core biopsy important after a dry tap?',options:['It shows overall cellularity and fibrous replacement that an aspirate may miss','It is only used to count circulating reticulocytes','It directly measures serum erythropoietin','It cannot identify marrow fibrosis'],answer:0,why:'Aspirate quality is poor in fibrosis; core histology can reveal hypocellularity, architectural changes and collagen replacing hematopoietic marrow.'}],differentials:[{name:'Myelofibrosis',tier:'best',support:'Pancytopenia, dry tap and core demonstrating fibrous marrow replacement',against:'Underlying driver is not established',test:'Core biopsy histopathology and secondary cause assessment'},{name:'Aplastic marrow',tier:'possible',support:'Pancytopenia and hypocellularity can occur',against:'Core specifically describes increased fibrous tissue',test:'Core architecture and fibrosis staining'},{name:'Myelophthisic infiltration',tier:'possible',support:'Marrow replacement can cause pancytopenia',against:'No neoplastic or granulomatous cells identified in core',test:'Full histopathology review for focal infiltrates'},{name:'Sampling failure alone',tier:'unlikely',support:'Initial aspirate was non-diagnostic',against:'Second attempt and core corroborate an intrinsic marrow abnormality',test:'Adequate core review'},{name:'Peripheral destruction/consumption only',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can decrease peripheral blood counts',against:'Marrow is hypocellular rather than appropriately responding',test:'Integrated marrow and peripheral trends'}]},
{id:'4.4',kind:'SBA',title:'Cat · Negative marrow iron stain interpretation',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 4.4 · CP4 feline marrow hemosiderin/Prussian blue slides',signal:'11-year-old castrated male domestic shorthair',history:'Chronic weight loss, mild anemia, no melena; fed a good-quality diet.',exam:'Thin, mildly pale gums, normal abdominal palpation.',panels:[{name:'Erythrogram and marrow',rows:[['HCT','27 %','30–45'],['MCV','44 fL','39–55'],['Marrow cellularity','Normal','Qualitative'],['M:E ratio','1.7','1.21–2.16'],['Prussian blue aspirate stain','No stainable iron','Species-specific finding']]}],smear:'Prussian blue stain shows no blue hemosiderin in macrophages. Orderly erythroid and granulocytic maturation.',photos:['Prussian blue marrow hemosiderin in species with normally stainable stores (CP4)'],questions:[{q:'Is a negative Prussian blue marrow iron stain diagnostic for iron deficiency in a cat?',options:['No; normal feline marrow aspirates generally lack stainable iron','Yes; absence always proves iron deficiency','Yes; marrow aplasia is confirmed','It proves iron overload'],answer:0,why:'The course specifically notes that stainable iron is not normally seen in feline marrow aspirates. A negative stain alone does not establish deficiency in cats.'},{q:'What is the best interpretation of the negative Prussian blue stain? (Original SBA)',options:['Iron deficiency anemia','Anemia of inflammatory disease','Normal finding in cat marrow aspirates, not evidence of iron deficiency','Hemochromatosis','Aplastic anemia'],answer:2,why:'Absent marrow iron staining is typical for cats and should not be used to diagnose iron deficiency. Marrow cellularity and M:E are within the provided normal descriptors/intervals.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'4.5',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · EPO treatment relapse and erythroid hypoplasia',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 4.5 · CP4 Recombinant Growth Factors; Erythropoietin',signal:'10-year-old spayed female Poodle with chronic kidney disease',history:'Treated non-regenerative anemia with recombinant human EPO for eight weeks. HCT improved initially, but weakness and pallor have returned.',exam:'Pale mucous membranes, HR 140/min, hydrated and normothermic.',panels:[{name:'CBC',rows:[['HCT','14 %','37–55'],['Absolute reticulocytes','4 ×10³/µL','10–110'],['WBC','9.2 ×10³/µL','6.0–17.0'],['Neutrophils','6.4 ×10³/µL','3.0–11.5'],['Platelets','310 ×10³/µL','200–500']]},{name:'Renal and marrow',rows:[['Creatinine','3.1 mg/dL','0.5–1.4'],['Marrow M:E ratio','8.0','0.75–2.53']]}],smear:'Erythroid lineage markedly reduced; granulocytes and megakaryocytes normal.',photos:['Erythroid maturation sequence (CP4 Erythropoiesis)','Dog marrow aspirate (CP4)'],questions:[{q:'What does the combination of severe anemia, reticulocytopenia and preserved WBC/platelets suggest?',options:['Selective failure of erythroid production','Global marrow pancytopenia','Strong erythrocyte regeneration','Primary thrombocytopenia'],answer:0,why:'HCT 14% with absolute reticulocytes 4 ×10³/µL is severe non-regenerative anemia. Other circulating cell lines and marrow precursors are maintained.'},{q:'How should M:E 8.0 be interpreted when the neutrophil lineage is normal?',options:['Relative rise from profound erythroid hypoplasia','Mandatory neutrophilic leukemia','Normal M:E for dogs','Proof of increased circulating reticulocytes'],answer:0,why:'Markedly reduced erythroid precursors raise the myeloid-to-erythroid ratio when myeloid production is relatively preserved. The treatment-timed relapse raises suspicion for anti-EPO immune responses.'}],differentials:[{name:'Anti-rhEPO antibody-mediated pure red cell aplasia',tier:'best',support:'Initial improvement then relapse after recombinant human EPO; selective erythroid hypoplasia',against:'Specific antibodies not documented',test:'Review treatment, consider anti-EPO antibody investigation and specialist evaluation'},{name:'Progression of CKD with reduced endogenous EPO',tier:'possible',support:'Known CKD and non-regenerative anemia',against:'Sudden selective erythroid suppression after recombinant EPO is more specific',test:'Serial renal, hematologic and clinical assessment'},{name:'Primary immune-mediated pure red cell aplasia',tier:'possible',support:'Selective erythroid deficiency',against:'Recent rhEPO exposure is a more compelling trigger',test:'Hematology assessment of immune/other causes'},{name:'Drug or infection-associated marrow suppression',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can alter hematopoiesis',against:'Other marrow lines remain normal',test:'Exposure and infection history as indicated'},{name:'Chronic hemorrhage',tier:'unlikely',support:'May decrease HCT',against:'No regenerative marrow response and no blood loss described',test:'Targeted bleeding work-up if warranted'}]}
,
{id:'5.1',kind:'DDx',title:'Cat · Methemoglobinemia and Heinz bodies',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 5.1 · CP5 slides 47–53 and CP6 slide 76',signal:'3-year-old neutered male domestic shorthair, indoor–outdoor',history:'Found four hours ago vomiting, drooling and breathing hard; owner uncertain about possible exposures.',exam:'HR 220/min, RR 60/min with open-mouth breathing, temperature 37.6 °C, chocolate-brown/cyanotic mucous membranes, facial and paw edema; blood remains brown on white paper.',panels:[{name:'CBC and oxidant markers',rows:[['HCT','28 %','30–45'],['Hemoglobin','9.0 g/dL','9.5–15.5'],['RBC','6.1 ×10⁶/µL','6.0–10.0'],['MCV','46 fL','39–55'],['MCHC','32.1 g/dL','31–35'],['Methemoglobin','45 % of total Hb','<1.5'],['Heinz-body RBCs (NMB)','30 %','Up to 5']]},{name:'Oxygenation and chemistry',rows:[['PaO₂ (room air)','96 mmHg','80–100'],['Pulse oximeter SpO₂','84 %','>95'],['ALT','190 U/L','25–100']]}],smear:'Heinz bodies (blue on new methylene blue), few eccentrocytes; no spherocytes or polychromasia yet in this acute presentation.',photos:['Feline Heinz bodies on Wright–Giemsa and new methylene blue stains (CP6 slide 75)','Heinz-body figure (CP5 slide 53)'],questions:[{q:'What explains brown blood and cyanosis despite a PaO₂ of 96 mmHg?',options:['Methemoglobin formation reduces hemoglobin oxygen carriage despite normal dissolved oxygen tension','A normal PaO₂ rules out dyshemoglobinemia','High PaO₂ confirms pulmonary edema as the sole process','Heinz bodies necessarily indicate iron deficiency'],answer:0,why:'Methemoglobin contains Fe³⁺, which cannot bind oxygen. Arterial PaO₂ reflects dissolved oxygen and may remain normal, while conventional pulse oximetry is unreliable in marked methemoglobinemia.'},{q:'What do 30% Heinz bodies and some eccentrocytes indicate?',options:['Marked oxidative injury to hemoglobin and erythrocyte membranes','Uncomplicated physiologic feline Heinz bodies','Pure blood loss without erythrocyte damage','Only a regenerative macrocytic anemia'],answer:0,why:'Heinz bodies are precipitated denatured hemoglobin. Eccentrocytes indicate oxidative membrane damage; together with methemoglobin 45%, these strongly support an acute oxidant insult.'}],differentials:[{name:'Acetaminophen-associated oxidant toxicosis',tier:'best',support:'Acute brown blood, methemoglobinemia, marked Heinz bodies, facial edema and ALT elevation',against:'Exposure has not yet been confirmed',test:'Immediate directed medication/exposure history and appropriate emergency toxicology assessment'},{name:'Other exogenous oxidant exposure (onion/garlic, propylene glycol, propofol, methylene blue)',tier:'possible',support:'Oxidant injuries can produce Heinz bodies and methemoglobinemia',against:'No exposure specifically documented',test:'Diet, medication, anesthetic and household exposure history'},{name:'Cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency',tier:'possible',support:'Can cause persistently elevated methemoglobin',against:'Acute onset and prominent Heinz bodies favor exogenous oxidant injury',test:'Prior history and methemoglobin/reductase evaluation if indicated'},{name:'Nitrite or nitrate exposure',tier:'possible',support:'Can increase methemoglobin',against:'Prominent Heinz bodies suggest broader oxidative damage',test:'Review water and fertilizer exposures'},{name:'Underlying disease with mild Heinz bodies',tier:'unlikely',support:'Some cats have Heinz bodies with systemic conditions',against:'Does not explain 45% methemoglobin and acute brown blood',test:'Consider underlying disease after stabilization and exposure investigation'}]},
{id:'5.2',kind:'SBA',title:'Dog · Pyruvate kinase deficiency and ATP loss',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 5.2 · CP5 slides 36–39',signal:'14-month-old neutered male Basenji',history:'Exercise intolerance since four months of age and occasional yellow tinge of gums; no drugs, toxin exposure or travel.',exam:'Pale and mildly icteric gums, HR 150/min, grade 2/6 systolic flow murmur, mild splenomegaly, temperature 38.7 °C.',panels:[{name:'CBC and regenerative response',rows:[['HCT','19 %','37–55'],['Hemoglobin','6.4 g/dL','12–18'],['RBC','2.4 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','80 fL','60–77'],['MCHC','33.7 g/dL','32–36'],['Reticulocytes','22 %','<1.5'],['Absolute reticulocytes','528,000 /µL','<60,000']]},{name:'Other testing',rows:[['Total bilirubin','1.6 mg/dL','0.1–0.3'],['Coombs test','Negative','Negative']]}],smear:'Marked polychromasia, macrocytosis and anisocytosis; no spherocytes, agglutination, Heinz bodies, eccentrocytes or organisms.',photos:['Glycolysis map with pyruvate kinase and PFK circled (CP5 slide 39)','Optional canine reticulocytosis smear (CP8)'],questions:[{q:'How would you classify this erythrogram?',options:['Marked regenerative anemia with macrocytosis','Severe non-regenerative normocytic anemia','Normal erythrogram with bilirubin artifact','Microcytic non-regenerative anemia'],answer:0,why:'HCT 19%, high MCV 80 fL, absolute reticulocytes 528,000/µL and polychromasia identify a strong regenerative response.'},{q:"Which mechanism best explains this dog's persistent severe regenerative anemia? (Original SBA)",options:['Autoantibodies to altered band 3 on old RBCs','Loss of pyruvate kinase activity decreases RBC ATP and shortens RBC lifespan','Loss of G6PD activity produces characteristic oxidative Heinz bodies','Loss of cytochrome b5 reductase causes methemoglobin accumulation','Loss of 2,3-DPG pathway prevents oxygen release'],answer:1,why:'A young Basenji with chronic strongly regenerative, Coombs-negative hemolytic anemia and no oxidant lesions is consistent with pyruvate kinase deficiency. Inadequate glycolytic ATP compromises red-cell membrane ion pumps and deformability; confirm by enzyme or genetic testing.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'5.3',kind:'DDx',title:'Horse · Oxidant hemolysis after wilted leaf exposure',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 5.3 · CP5 slides 40–46, 53–54; CP6 slides 61–62',signal:'9-year-old Quarter Horse mare',history:'Two days of depression and anorexia, red-brown urine this morning; wilted tree leaves found in paddock after summer storm, tree species unknown.',exam:'HR 70/min, RR 36/min, temperature 38.2 °C; icteric muddy-brown mucous membranes, CRT 3 seconds, weak.',panels:[{name:'Erythrogram and plasma',rows:[['HCT','14 %','32–48'],['Hemoglobin','5.6 g/dL','11–19'],['RBC','3.0 ×10⁶/µL','6.5–12.5'],['MCV','47 fL','37–49'],['MCHC','40.0 g/dL','34–39'],['Methemoglobin','38 %','<1.5'],['Plasma color','Red-brown','Clear to pale yellow']]},{name:'Chemistry and urinalysis',rows:[['Total bilirubin','6.5 mg/dL','1.0–2.0'],['BUN','52 mg/dL','11–27'],['Creatinine','3.8 mg/dL','1.2–1.9'],['Urine dipstick blood','3+; no RBCs in sediment','Negative']]}],smear:'Numerous eccentrocytes, Heinz bodies, pyknocytes and ghost cells; background hemolyzed.',photos:['Eccentrocytes in equine smear (CP5 slide 54 and CP6 slide 62)'],questions:[{q:'Which morphologic changes most strongly identify oxidant injury?',options:['Eccentrocytes and Heinz bodies','A normal blood film','Spherocytes only with autoagglutination','Rouleaux without erythrocyte damage'],answer:0,why:'Oxidative membrane injury produces eccentrocytes/pyknocytes; denatured hemoglobin precipitates into Heinz bodies. These changes support oxidant injury rather than an isolated regenerative anemia.'},{q:'What does positive urine blood with no sediment RBCs suggest in this setting?',options:['Pigmenturia consistent with hemoglobinuria during intravascular hemolysis','Confirmed uncomplicated cystitis','Only lower urinary tract hemorrhage','Dipstick blood excludes hemolysis'],answer:0,why:'Urine heme-positive without RBCs suggests free hemoglobin or myoglobin; concurrent severe anemia, hemolyzed plasma and oxidant RBC damage support hemoglobinuria in this source case.'}],differentials:[{name:'Red maple (Acer rubrum) leaf toxicosis',tier:'best',support:'Wilted foliage, methemoglobinemia, eccentrocytes, Heinz bodies and hemolysis',against:'Tree species not yet identified',test:'Identify and investigate the consumed tree/leaves'},{name:'Hereditary G6PD or glutathione reductase deficiency',tier:'possible',support:'Red-cell antioxidant-pathway defects cause oxidative injury',against:'Adult acute episode after leaf exposure better fits an acquired trigger',test:'Erythrocyte enzyme testing if exposure is excluded or episodes recur'},{name:'Phenothiazine-associated oxidative injury',tier:'possible',support:'Can generate equine Heinz bodies',against:'No drug exposure supplied',test:'Detailed medication and dewormer history'},{name:'Immune-mediated hemolysis',tier:'unlikely',support:'Could cause anemia and icterus',against:'Oxidant lesions dominate; no spherocytes/agglutination reported',test:'Immune hemolysis work-up if new evidence arises'},{name:'Theileria equi infection',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can cause equine hemolysis',against:'No intraerythrocytic parasites observed; exposure scenario favors toxicosis',test:'Exposure assessment and infectious testing when indicated'}]},
{id:'5.4',kind:'SBA',title:'Cat · Type B transfusion compatibility',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 5.4 · CP5 slides 28–34',signal:'6-year-old neutered male domestic shorthair',history:'Five days of lethargy and black stools; no previous transfusion or vaccine reaction; FeLV/FIV negative.',exam:'Pale mucous membranes, HR 210/min, weak pulses, temperature 37.4 °C; owner requests transfusion.',panels:[{name:'CBC and proteins',rows:[['HCT','11 %','30–45'],['Hemoglobin','3.6 g/dL','9.5–15.5'],['RBC','2.4 ×10⁶/µL','6.0–10.0'],['MCV','46 fL','39–55'],['MCHC','32.7 g/dL','31–35'],['Total protein','4.2 g/dL','6.0–8.0']]},{name:'Feline blood typing',rows:[['AB card test','Agglutination in B well only; none in A well','Type B']]}],smear:'No morphology specified; interpretation is based on feline AB card typing.',photos:['Feline AB card test showing type B result (CP5 slides 33–34)'],questions:[{q:'What feline blood type is indicated by agglutination in only the B test well?',options:['Type B','Type A','Type AB','Blood typing cannot be interpreted from the card'],answer:0,why:'In the supplied card result, B-well agglutination without A-well agglutination identifies feline type B.'},{q:'The cat is blood type B. Which donor choice is correct? (Original SBA)',options:['Type A donor, as type B cats have only weak anti-A antibodies','Type A donor infused slowly over 8 hours','Type B donor confirmed compatible by crossmatch','Any donor because cats lack clinically important natural antibodies','DEA 1-negative donor'],answer:2,why:'Type B cats typically have strong naturally occurring anti-A antibodies; mismatched type A RBCs may cause a severe acute reaction even on first exposure. Select a type B donor and confirm compatibility by crossmatch.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'5.5',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Heinz-body anemia after shared stew',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 5.5 · CP5 slides 46, 53, 55–62; CP6 slide 76',signal:'4-year-old spayed female Shiba Inu',history:'Two days of weakness, pallor and red-brown urine; table scraps and a large pot of shared stew three days ago; no medications.',exam:'HR 150/min, pale and mildly icteric mucous membranes, temperature 38.9 °C, mild splenomegaly.',panels:[{name:'Erythrogram and oxidant markers',rows:[['HCT','24 %','37–55'],['Hemoglobin','8.6 g/dL','12–18'],['RBC','3.4 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','70 fL','60–77'],['MCHC','35.8 g/dL','32–36'],['Reticulocytes','4.5 %','<1.5'],['Absolute reticulocytes','153,000 /µL','<60,000'],['Heinz-body RBCs (NMB)','25 %','<5']]},{name:'Plasma, bilirubin and urine',rows:[['Plasma color','Pink-red','Clear to pale yellow'],['Total bilirubin','2.4 mg/dL','0.1–0.3'],['Urine dipstick blood','3+; no RBCs in sediment','Negative']]}],smear:'Heinz bodies, eccentrocytes, ghost cells and polychromasia; no spherocytes or agglutination.',photos:['Heinz bodies and eccentrocytes in dog (CP6 slides 74–76 and 59)','Optional icteric mucous membranes (CP5 slide 62)'],questions:[{q:'Which anemia mechanism is indicated by Heinz bodies, eccentrocytes and polychromasia?',options:['Oxidative red-cell damage with a regenerative response','Purely non-regenerative marrow disease','Normal erythrogram','Iron deficiency without hemolysis'],answer:0,why:'Heinz bodies and eccentrocytes reflect oxidant injury; polychromasia and elevated reticulocytes demonstrate regeneration after erythrocyte loss.'},{q:'Does this case support intravascular, extravascular or both routes of hemolysis?',options:['Both: pink plasma/hemoglobinuria and icterus/splenomegaly','Only intravascular: bilirubin cannot rise','Only extravascular: free plasma hemoglobin is absent','Neither, because RBCs are intact'],answer:0,why:'Pink-red plasma and urine heme without RBCs suggest free hemoglobin from intravascular lysis. Elevated bilirubin, icterus and splenomegaly indicate macrophage clearance of damaged RBCs (extravascular hemolysis).'}],differentials:[{name:'Allium (onion/garlic) oxidative toxicosis',tier:'best',support:'Stew exposure, Heinz bodies, eccentrocytes, regenerative anemia and pigmenturia',against:'Ingredients are not confirmed yet',test:'Obtain recipe/ingredient and exposure history'},{name:'Zinc toxicosis',tier:'possible',support:'Can cause canine Heinz-body hemolysis and pigmenturia',against:'No metal ingestion history',test:'Foreign body/exposure history and imaging if indicated'},{name:'Other oxidants (acetaminophen, naphthalene, propofol, skunk musk)',tier:'possible',support:'Other oxidant triggers can produce Heinz bodies',against:'No implicated exposure supplied',test:'Directed drug and household toxin inventory'},{name:'Immune-mediated hemolytic anemia',tier:'unlikely',support:'Regenerative anemia and bilirubin elevation could fit',against:'No spherocytes or agglutination; marked oxidant lesions favor exposure',test:'Further immune testing only if clinical suspicion persists'},{name:'Pyruvate kinase deficiency',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can cause hemolytic anemia',against:'Usually chronic/younger onset and not associated with oxidant lesions',test:'Consider genetic/enzyme testing in recurrent unexplained cases'}]}
,
{id:'6.1',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Fragmentation anemia and consumptive coagulopathy',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 6.1 · CP6 slides 51–56, 63',signal:'9-year-old castrated male German Shepherd',history:'Collapsed yesterday and recovered; pale gums today and abdomen appears larger.',exam:'HR 170/min, weak pulses, pale mucous membranes, abdominal fluid wave and a few gingival petechiae.',panels:[{name:'CBC and blood-film clues',rows:[['HCT','22 %','37–55'],['Hemoglobin','7.4 g/dL','12–18'],['RBC','3.3 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','67 fL','60–77'],['MCHC','33.6 g/dL','32–36'],['Absolute reticulocytes','99,000 /µL','<60,000'],['Platelets','38 ×10³/µL','200–500']]},{name:'Coagulation and proteins',rows:[['PT','14 s','6–9'],['aPTT','31 s','12–17'],['Fibrinogen','90 mg/dL','150–400'],['D-dimer','>2,000 ng/mL','<250'],['Total protein','5.0 g/dL','5.4–7.5']]}],smear:'Schistocytes, keratocytes, acanthocytes and polychromasia; a few spherocyte-like cells; few platelets.',photos:['Schistocytes and keratocytes in canine smear (CP6 slides 51–53)','Iron-deficiency RBC fragments as comparison (CP6 slide 56)'],questions:[{q:'Which smear pattern best identifies mechanical erythrocyte fragmentation?',options:['Schistocytes and keratocytes','Isolated rouleaux','Only Howell–Jolly bodies','Uniform mature erythrocytes'],answer:0,why:'Schistocytes are RBC fragments, strongly indicating fragmentation. Keratocytes and acanthocytes can accompany mechanical or microangiopathic injury, although none alone proves the underlying trigger.'},{q:'What do low platelets, prolonged PT/aPTT, low fibrinogen and high D-dimer suggest together?',options:['Consumptive coagulopathy compatible with disseminated intravascular coagulation','Normal hemostasis','Isolated immune-mediated thrombocytopenia with normal coagulation','Only vitamin K–independent erythrocyte regeneration'],answer:0,why:'Platelets 38 ×10³/µL, PT 14 s, aPTT 31 s, fibrinogen 90 mg/dL and D-dimer >2,000 ng/mL together support consumption and fibrinolysis, rather than an isolated red-cell disorder.'}],differentials:[{name:'Hemangiosarcoma-associated DIC',tier:'best',support:'Collapse, likely cavitary hemorrhage, fragmentation, thrombocytopenia and consumptive coagulation pattern',against:'Underlying mass or neoplasm has not been confirmed',test:'Abdominal ultrasound, echocardiography and assessment/sampling of effusion where appropriate'},{name:'DIC secondary to another severe disease (sepsis, neoplasia, heat stroke)',tier:'possible',support:'Coagulation and RBC fragmentation pattern is compatible with systemic DIC',against:'No alternative primary trigger has been identified',test:'Directed diagnostic search for inflammatory, neoplastic or thermal triggers'},{name:'Heartworm caval syndrome',tier:'possible',support:'Mechanical erythrocyte injury can cause fragmentation',against:'Clinical evidence for caval syndrome not described',test:'Heartworm testing and cardiac evaluation when indicated'},{name:'Glomerular, valvular, vascular or hepatosplenic disease',tier:'possible',support:'Other described causes of fragmentation',against:'Consumptive coagulopathy and abdominal fluid favor a systemic hemorrhagic process',test:'Urinalysis, chemistry, auscultation and imaging'},{name:'Primary immune-mediated hemolytic anemia',tier:'unlikely',support:'Pallor and a few spherocyte-like RBCs are nonspecific',against:'Schistocytes and abnormal coagulation tests are more diagnostic of fragmentation with consumption',test:'Immune hemolysis testing only if distinct supportive evidence emerges'}]},
{id:'6.2',kind:'SBA',title:'Dog · Nucleated RBCs, basophilic stippling and lead',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 6.2 · CP6 slides 64–69, 78',signal:'8-month-old neutered male Labrador mix',history:'Recently adopted; lives in a house under renovation. Four days of vomiting/diarrhea followed by two seizures this morning.',exam:'HR 130/min, temperature 38.5 °C, mildly pale mucous membranes and hypersalivation; no fever or icterus.',panels:[{name:'Erythrogram and nucleated RBCs',rows:[['HCT','33 %','37–55'],['Hemoglobin','11.3 g/dL','12–18'],['RBC','4.9 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','67 fL','60–77'],['MCHC','34.2 g/dL','32–36'],['Reticulocytes','0.4 %','<1.5'],['Absolute reticulocytes','19,600 /µL','<60,000'],['nRBC / 100 WBC','120','0–1']]},{name:'WBC correction and toxicology',rows:[['Analyzer WBC','14.0 ×10³/µL','6–17'],['Corrected WBC','6.4 ×10³/µL','6–17'],['Blood lead','85 µg/dL','<10']]}],smear:'More nucleated RBCs than leukocytes; mild basophilic stippling; no polychromasia or schistocytes.',photos:['Lead-toxicity smear with numerous nRBC and stippling (CP6 slide 65)','Basophilic stippling image (CP6 slide 78)'],questions:[{q:'Calculate the corrected WBC from analyzer WBC 14.0 and 120 nRBC per 100 WBC.',options:['6.4 ×10³/µL','14.0 ×10³/µL','30.8 ×10³/µL','1.2 ×10³/µL'],answer:0,why:'Corrected WBC = analyzer WBC × 100/(100 + nRBC per 100 WBC) = 14.0 × 100/220 = 6.36 ≈ 6.4 ×10³/µL. Numerous nucleated RBCs can inflate analyzer WBC counts.'},{q:'Which interpretation fits best? (Original SBA)',options:['Strongly regenerative anemia because nRBC and stippling prove regeneration','Lead toxicity with nRBC and stippling, and mild non-regenerative anemia','Heat stroke with fragmentation','Normal mammalian circulating nRBC as in birds','Hereditary dyserythropoiesis'],answer:1,why:'GI and neurological signs after renovation exposure, blood lead 85 µg/dL, prominent nRBC and basophilic stippling with inadequate reticulocyte regeneration favor lead poisoning. Nucleated RBCs alone do not prove regeneration.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'6.3',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Eccentrocytes in diabetic ketoacidosis',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 6.3 · CP6 slides 57–61; adapted from diabetic dog with infection slide 58',signal:'7-year-old spayed female Miniature Poodle',history:'Three weeks of PU/PD, two days of vomiting and a foul-smelling oral abscess. No known toxin exposure.',exam:'Approximately 8% dehydrated, HR 140/min, deep rapid breaths, ketotic breath, temperature 39.6 °C, cataracts.',panels:[{name:'CBC and serum glucose',rows:[['HCT','31 %','37–55'],['Hemoglobin','10.7 g/dL','12–18'],['RBC','4.5 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','69 fL','60–77'],['MCHC','34.5 g/dL','32–36'],['WBC','26.5 ×10³/µL','6–17'],['Glucose','548 mg/dL','65–120']]},{name:'Electrolytes and urine',rows:[['Sodium','142 mEq/L','140–150'],['Potassium','4.4 mEq/L','3.7–5.4'],['Chloride','102 mEq/L','106–116'],['Bicarbonate','10 mEq/L','18–24'],['Anion gap (Na + K − Cl − HCO₃)','34.4','12–24'],['Urine ketones','3+','Negative']]}],smear:'Approximately 6% eccentrocytes with some echinocytes and pyknocytes; no Heinz bodies, spherocytes or polychromasia.',photos:['Eccentrocyte, echinocyte and pyknocyte in diabetic dog (CP6 slide 58)','Normal RBC alongside eccentrocyte (CP6 slide 59)'],questions:[{q:'What is the likely cause of eccentrocytes in this clinical context?',options:['Endogenous oxidant stress from ketoacidotic diabetes with infection','A normal RBC feature in all dogs','Pure regenerative erythropoiesis','Proof of hemotropic mycoplasma infection'],answer:0,why:'Eccentrocytes are associated with membrane oxidative damage. Marked hyperglycemia, ketonuria, low bicarbonate and high anion gap with infection support DKA-associated endogenous oxidant stress.'},{q:'Using the supplied electrolyte values, what is the calculated anion gap?',options:['34.4 mEq/L','14.4 mEq/L','24.0 mEq/L','58.4 mEq/L'],answer:0,why:'Source uses Na + K − Cl − HCO₃: 142 + 4.4 − 102 − 10 = 34.4 mEq/L, above the supplied interval of 12–24.'}],differentials:[{name:'Endogenous oxidants from diabetic ketoacidosis and infection',tier:'best',support:'Hyperglycemia, ketones, high anion gap, low bicarbonate, infection and mild eccentrocytosis',against:'No direct quantitative oxidant measurement provided',test:'DKA assessment, serial metabolic monitoring and evaluation of oral abscess'},{name:'Neoplasia-associated endogenous oxidants (including lymphoma)',tier:'possible',support:'Neoplasia can accompany eccentrocytosis',against:'No mass/lymphadenopathy described and a clear metabolic trigger is present',test:'Physical examination and directed cytology or imaging when indicated'},{name:'Exogenous oxidants (Allium, drugs, acetaminophen, propofol)',tier:'possible',support:'Can produce oxidant RBC changes',against:'No exposure elicited',test:'Focused medication, diet and toxin history'},{name:'Vitamin K antagonist exposure',tier:'unlikely',support:'Included as a differential in the source lecture',against:'No bleeding history or prolongation of PT described',test:'Exposure and coagulation assessment if indicated'},{name:'Hereditary G6PD/glutathione reductase deficiency',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can cause erythrocyte oxidant susceptibility',against:'Source emphasizes horses and acute DKA-associated pattern better fits this dog',test:'Consider rare hereditary causes if persistent unexplained changes'}]},
{id:'6.4',kind:'SBA',title:'Dog · Howell–Jolly versus Heinz after splenectomy',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 6.4 · CP6 slides 70–76',signal:'10-year-old neutered male German Shepherd',history:'Splenectomy three months ago for benign splenic hematoma; healthy at pre-dental examination, no drugs.',exam:'Normal exam; HR 90/min, pink mucous membranes, temperature 38.4 °C.',panels:[{name:'CBC',rows:[['HCT','41 %','37–55'],['Hemoglobin','14.2 g/dL','12–18'],['RBC','5.9 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','69 fL','60–77'],['MCHC','34.6 g/dL','32–36'],['Absolute reticulocytes','47,200 /µL','<60,000'],['Platelets','640 ×10³/µL','200–500']]},{name:'Chemistry',rows:[['Total bilirubin','0.2 mg/dL','0.1–0.3']]}],smear:'Some RBCs contain small round dark-purple Howell–Jolly bodies; a few show pale projecting Heinz bodies (~3%); no polychromasia or organisms.',photos:['Canine post-splenectomy Howell–Jolly bodies (CP6 slide 72)','Howell–Jolly versus Heinz on Wright–Giemsa and reticulocyte stain (CP6 slide 74)'],questions:[{q:'Which visual distinction best separates Howell–Jolly from Heinz bodies?',options:['Howell–Jolly bodies are nuclear remnants staining dark; Heinz bodies are denatured hemoglobin highlighted by supravital stain','Both represent intracellular bacteria','Howell–Jolly bodies are extracellular RBC parasites','Neither can appear after splenectomy'],answer:0,why:'Howell–Jolly bodies are DNA remnants staining dark purple on Romanowsky stains. Heinz bodies are oxidized hemoglobin, often pale on Wright–Giemsa and highlighted on new methylene blue.'},{q:'What is the correct interpretation of these inclusions? (Original SBA)',options:['Hemotropic mycoplasma infection requiring doxycycline','Lead toxicity requiring chelation','Expected after splenectomy; no treatment, and reticulocyte staining distinguishes Heinz from Howell–Jolly bodies','Regenerative anemia, proven by Howell–Jolly bodies','Babesia gibsoni infection requiring imidocarb'],answer:2,why:'The spleen helps remove RBC inclusions. With normal HCT and no reticulocytosis, occasional Howell–Jolly bodies and a few Heinz bodies after splenectomy do not alone establish disease requiring treatment.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'6.5',kind:'DDx',title:'Cat · Fluctuating RBC-surface organisms and hemolysis',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 6.5 · CP6 slides 79–98, 90–97',signal:'3-year-old neutered male outdoor domestic shorthair',history:'Outdoor cat that fights; lethargic and inappetent for six days, no travel, unvaccinated for FeLV.',exam:'Temperature 40.4 °C, HR 220/min, pale/icteric mucous membranes and mild splenomegaly; no tick found.',panels:[{name:'CBC and regeneration',rows:[['HCT','15 %','30–45'],['Hemoglobin','4.9 g/dL','9.5–15.5'],['RBC','3.3 ×10⁶/µL','6.0–10.0'],['MCV','45 fL','39–55'],['MCHC','32.7 g/dL','31–35'],['Aggregate reticulocytes','99,000 /µL','<15,000']]},{name:'Chemistry and infection testing',rows:[['Total bilirubin','3.1 mg/dL','0.1–0.3'],['Total protein','7.6 g/dL','6.0–8.0'],['FeLV antigen / FIV antibody','Negative','Negative']]}],smear:'9 AM smear appears clear; at 10:30 AM small coccoid, ring and chain-like structures are seen on the OUTSIDE of RBCs. Polychromasia and anisocytosis; no intraerythrocytic inclusions or WBC organisms.',photos:['M. haemofelis RBC surfaces at 9 AM vs 10:30 AM (CP6 slide 96)','M. haemofelis versus M. haemominutum comparison (CP6 slide 94)'],questions:[{q:'Why can the first smear be negative and the second show organisms?',options:['Hemotropic mycoplasma parasitemia may fluctuate, so one negative smear cannot rule it out','RBC-surface mycoplasmas must always be visible','All feline RBC organisms are intracellular protozoa','FeLV status alone determines smear appearance'],answer:0,why:'The source contrasts 9 AM and 10:30 AM smears. Hemoplasmas can be intermittently evident on blood films; PCR is used for confirmation.'},{q:'Where are hemotropic mycoplasmas located compared with Babesia/Cytauxzoon?',options:['On the RBC surface rather than inside RBCs','Exclusively within neutrophils','Only within platelets','Inside the RBC nucleus'],answer:0,why:'Hemotropic mycoplasmas attach to erythrocyte surfaces. Babesia, Cytauxzoon and several other blood agents are intraerythrocytic; location supports the differential but does not replace confirmatory testing.'}],differentials:[{name:'Mycoplasma haemofelis infection',tier:'best',support:'Regenerative hemolytic anemia, RBC-surface coccoid organisms, outdoor/fighting history and fluctuation between smears',against:'Morphology alone cannot reliably specify hemoplasma species',test:'Feline hemoplasma PCR; review repeated smears in clinical context'},{name:'Mycoplasma haemominutum infection',tier:'possible',support:'Another feline RBC-surface hemoplasma',against:'Often milder or subclinical compared with this severe anemia',test:'Species-specific PCR where available'},{name:'Cytauxzoon felis',tier:'possible',support:'Fever, icterus and feline hemolytic disease could fit',against:'No intraerythrocytic forms or macrophage schizonts described; organisms appear on RBC surfaces',test:'Targeted Cytauxzoon diagnostics if epidemiology warrants'},{name:'Babesia felis or similar intraerythrocytic protozoa',tier:'unlikely',support:'Potential hemolysis',against:'No organisms seen within erythrocytes; geography and smear pattern less supportive',test:'Directed molecular testing when relevant'},{name:'Bartonella, precipitate or Heinz-body look-alikes',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can be mistaken visually for RBC-associated structures',against:'Blood film repeatedly shows external coccoid/chain forms in compatible hemolytic disease',test:'Repeat stained film and PCR-based organism detection'},{name:'Primary IMHA',tier:'unlikely',support:'Regenerative anemia and hyperbilirubinemia occur with immune destruction',against:'RBC-surface organisms offer a more specific infectious explanation',test:'Evaluate immune hemolysis if concurrent features suggest it'}]}
,
{id:'7.1',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Microcytic hypochromic anemia and melena',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 7.1 · CP7 slides 23, 25, 27, 35, 46, 49–50 (reference intervals source-marked Added)',signal:'7-year-old castrated male Labrador',history:'Six weeks of intermittent black tarry stool, weight loss and exercise intolerance; not dewormed; no NSAIDs or steroids.',exam:'HR 150/min, pale gums, no icterus, temperature 38.5 °C, no neurologic signs.',panels:[{name:'Erythrogram and platelets',rows:[['HCT','20 %','37–55'],['Hemoglobin','5.8 g/dL','12–18'],['RBC','4.0 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','50 fL','60–77'],['MCH','14.5 pg','19.5–24.5'],['MCHC','29 g/dL','32–36'],['RDW','21 %','12–15'],['Absolute reticulocytes','72 ×10³/µL','<60'],['Platelets','680 ×10³/µL','200–500']]},{name:'Iron and protein evaluation',rows:[['Reticulocyte hemoglobin content','17 pg','22–28'],['Serum iron','28 µg/dL','90–230'],['Total plasma protein','5.0 g/dL','5.5–7.5'],['Plasma appearance','Colorless','Colorless']]}],smear:'Microcytes and hypochromic cells with prominent central pallor, fragments, mild polychromasia and thrombocytosis.',photos:['Microcytic hypochromic erythrocytes in iron deficiency (CP7 slide 27, right panel)'],questions:[{q:'Explain this erythrogram: what pattern do MCV 50, MCH 14.5, MCHC 29 and RDW 21 identify?',options:['Microcytic, hypochromic anemia with anisocytosis','Macrocytic, hyperchromic erythrocytosis','Normal canine erythrogram','Normocytic hemoconcentration'],answer:0,why:'Low MCV means small cells, while low MCH and MCHC support reduced RBC hemoglobin content/concentration; increased RDW reflects cell-size variability. HCT and Hb are markedly reduced.'},{q:'Why is the absolute reticulocyte count of 72 ×10³/µL not reassuring at HCT 20%?',options:['It is inadequate for the severity of anemia; depleted iron limits new hemoglobin production','It proves a fully appropriate strong regenerative response','Reticulocyte numbers cannot be assessed in dogs','It shows normal RBC mass'],answer:0,why:'Although slightly above the source reference cutoff (<60 ×10³/µL), an absolute count of 72 is small relative to an HCT of 20%. Low reticulocyte Hb content and iron indicate iron-restricted erythropoiesis.'}],differentials:[{name:'Chronic gastrointestinal blood loss with iron deficiency',tier:'best',support:'Melena, microcytosis, hypochromia, high RDW, low iron/reticulocyte Hb and low TPP',against:'The precise source of bleeding is not yet demonstrated',test:'Fecal examination, GI evaluation and ferritin/iron assessment guided by history'},{name:'Anemia of inflammatory disease',tier:'possible',support:'Serum iron can be low from hepcidin-mediated sequestration',against:'Severe microcytosis and low reticulocyte Hb with melena better support true iron depletion',test:'Inflammatory assessment and iron-store evaluation'},{name:'Portosystemic shunt',tier:'possible',support:'Can cause canine microcytosis',against:'Severe anemia, low TPP and melena favor chronic blood loss',test:'Bile acids and hepatic vascular evaluation if indicated'},{name:'Heme synthesis inhibition (lead, drugs, vitamin B6 deficiency)',tier:'unlikely',support:'Can cause altered hemoglobin production',against:'No exposure or neurologic signs and no basophilic stippling described',test:'Directed toxin/medication history if other clues develop'},{name:'Breed-associated microcytosis',tier:'unlikely',support:'Certain Asian dog breeds may have small RBCs without disease',against:'Labrador with severe anemia and melena, not an incidental breed-associated finding',test:'Compare prior CBCs and assess the bleeding source'}]},
{id:'7.2',kind:'SBA',title:'Sheep · Very high MCHC during intravascular hemolysis',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 7.2 · CP7 slides 3, 7, 28–33, 55 (source-marked reference intervals Added)',signal:'2-year-old Suffolk ewe',history:'Depressed after transport stress; flock fed mineral-rich cattle ration.',exam:'HR 130/min, pale and icteric mucous membranes, dark red-brown urine and brownish blood.',panels:[{name:'Erythrogram',rows:[['HCT','15 %','27–45'],['RBC','4.5 ×10⁶/µL','9–15'],['MCV','33 fL','28–40'],['Hemoglobin','6.9 g/dL','9–15'],['MCHC','46 g/dL','31–34'],['Absolute reticulocytes','0','0']]},{name:'Plasma and chemistry',rows:[['Plasma appearance','Red','Colorless'],['Total bilirubin','3.2 mg/dL','0.1–0.5']]}],smear:'Anisocytosis and ghost cells, without polychromasia or agglutination.',photos:['Hemolyzed plasma in microhematocrit tube (CP7 slide 7)','MCHC and hemolysis artifact graphic (CP7 slide 29)'],questions:[{q:'What makes MCHC 46 g/dL suspicious rather than evidence of truly hyperchromic erythrocytes?',options:['It exceeds plausible cellular hemoglobin concentration and suggests analytical interference','It proves iron-deficient microcytes','A high MCHC always means regeneration','Sheep normally have MCHC 46'],answer:0,why:'Extremely high MCHC is generally an artifact. Red plasma indicates free hemoglobin, which contributes to measured Hb without being confined to intact counted erythrocytes.'},{q:'The analyzer reports MCHC 46 g/dL in this anemic sheep. What is the best explanation? (Original SBA)',options:['Hereditary stomatocytosis causing swollen cells','Free hemoglobin in plasma from intravascular hemolysis is measured as Hb but is not inside counted RBCs','Strong reticulocyte response producing macrocytic hypochromic cells','Chronic iron deficiency','Persistent hyponatremia'],answer:1,why:'Calculated MCHC = 6.9/15 × 100 = 46 g/dL. Red plasma and dark urine suggest intravascular hemolysis; free hemoglobin falsely elevates the measured Hb-to-HCT relationship. The source highlights copper toxicosis as a possible trigger in sheep fed cattle minerals, but the exposure is not confirmed.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'7.3',kind:'DDx',title:'Dog · Hypernatremia and spurious macrocytosis',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 7.3 · CP7 slides 21, 22, 25, 33, 35 (source-marked reference intervals Added)',signal:'8-year-old spayed female Labrador',history:'Three days of vomiting and diarrhea without drinking; CBC two months ago showed MCV 70 fL.',exam:'Approximately 8% dehydrated, tacky mucous membranes, HR 130/min, bright and responsive, no pallor or icterus.',panels:[{name:'Analyzer and spun measurements',rows:[['Analyzer HCT','53 %','37–55'],['Spun PCV','47 %','37–55'],['RBC','6.4 ×10⁶/µL','5.5–8.5'],['MCV','83 fL','60–77'],['Hemoglobin','16.4 g/dL','12–18'],['Analyzer MCHC','31 g/dL','32–36'],['Reticulocytes','0.5 %','<1']]},{name:'Electrolytes and protein',rows:[['Sodium','168 mEq/L','145–154'],['Chloride','130 mEq/L','108–118'],['Total plasma protein','8.0 g/dL','5.5–7.5']]}],smear:'Erythrocytes appear normal in size and shape; no polychromasia, stomatocytes or agglutination.',photos:['RBC swelling when hypernatremic sample enters lower-osmolality analyzer diluent (CP7 slide 22)'],questions:[{q:'What discrepancy argues the high MCV may not reflect true macrocytosis?',options:['Analyzer HCT 53% differs from spun PCV 47%, with normal smear and no reticulocytosis','The patient has HCT below the reference interval','A prior MCV is unavailable','Marked polychromasia establishes regeneration'],answer:0,why:'RBC count × MCV / 10 yields electronic HCT ≈53%; spun PCV 47% is lower. The smear is not macrocytic, and the dog had MCV 70 fL previously.'},{q:'If hemoglobin is 16.4 g/dL and spun PCV is 47%, what is MCHC using spun PCV?',options:['About 35 g/dL, within the supplied canine range','About 46 g/dL','About 24 g/dL','About 53 g/dL'],answer:0,why:'MCHC = Hb/PCV × 100 = 16.4/47 × 100 = 34.9 g/dL, normal. The analyzer MCHC of 31 results from its inflated calculated hematocrit.'}],differentials:[{name:'Hypernatremia-related analyzer macrocytosis artifact',tier:'best',support:'Sodium 168, inflated electronic HCT, normal spun PCV-based MCHC and unremarkable smear',against:'Must confirm on repeat measurement after correcting the underlying electrolyte disorder',test:'Compare spun PCV/smear with analyzer indices and reassess after safe sodium correction'},{name:'Regenerative macrocytic anemia',tier:'unlikely',support:'Regeneration can increase MCV',against:'No anemia, no polychromasia and reticulocytes 0.5%',test:'Smear and reticulocyte reassessment if indicated'},{name:'Hereditary stomatocytosis',tier:'possible',support:'Can cause apparent macrocytosis and low MCHC',against:'No stomatocytes and older MCV was normal',test:'Re-examine blood film and prior CBC'},{name:'Autoagglutination',tier:'unlikely',support:'Clumping can disrupt analyzer indices',against:'No agglutination, and it commonly elevates rather than lowers apparent MCHC',test:'Saline assessment if smear suspicious'},{name:'Prolonged sample storage swelling',tier:'possible',support:'Delayed analysis can alter cell volume',against:'Source reports sample processed within two hours and marked hypernatremia is present',test:'Confirm sample handling and recollect if needed'},{name:'Breed-associated macrocytosis',tier:'unlikely',support:'Seen with some breeds',against:'Dog is a Labrador and past MCV was normal',test:'Review breed and longitudinal CBC'}]},
{id:'7.4',kind:'SBA',title:'Cat · Acetaminophen, cyanosis and methemoglobin',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 7.4 · CP7 slides 28, 51–57 (source-marked reference intervals Added)',signal:'2-year-old neutered male domestic shorthair',history:'Owner gave an extra-strength acetaminophen tablet for limping approximately eight hours earlier; vomiting since.',exam:'Brown-gray mucous membranes, facial and paw edema, HR 220/min, RR 60/min, temperature 37.3 °C, room-air SpO₂ 84%.',panels:[{name:'CBC and hemoglobin',rows:[['HCT','34 %','30–45'],['RBC','7.2 ×10⁶/µL','6–10'],['MCV','47 fL','39–55'],['Hemoglobin','12.9 g/dL','8–15'],['MCHC','38 g/dL','30–36']]},{name:'Oxygenation and blood color',rows:[['PaO₂','96 mmHg','80–100'],['Methemoglobin','58 %','<2'],['Blood color','Chocolate brown, stays brown on filter paper','Red']]}],smear:'Numerous Heinz bodies on new methylene blue stain and eccentrocytes.',photos:['Methemoglobin spot test or brown versus normal blood (CP7 slides 52, 54)'],questions:[{q:'How can a cat have a normal PaO₂ but severe tissue hypoxia in methemoglobinemia?',options:['PaO₂ measures dissolved oxygen; Fe³⁺ methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen normally','PaO₂ measures all hemoglobin-bound oxygen','A normal PaO₂ excludes dyshemoglobinemia','Pulse oximetry precisely measures saturation in severe methemoglobinemia'],answer:0,why:'PaO₂ 96 mmHg represents dissolved arterial oxygen. Methemoglobin 58% markedly reduces functional oxygen-carrying capacity despite normal PaO₂; conventional pulse oximetry is unreliable.'},{q:'Which finding best shows that cyanosis is due to methemoglobinemia rather than hypoxemia? (Original SBA)',options:['Low PaO₂ on arterial gas','Normal PaO₂ with brown-gray mucosa and blood that remains brown on the methemoglobin spot test','Positive direct antiglobulin test','Low serum ferritin','Increased serum EPO'],answer:1,why:'Normal arterial oxygen tension together with chocolate-brown blood and elevated methemoglobin is characteristic. Acetaminophen oxidant exposure and Heinz bodies support the mechanism.'}],differentials:[]},
{id:'7.5',kind:'DDx',title:'Cat · Coombs-positive hemolysis with RBC-surface organisms',source:'Cases_Part1.docx · Case 7.5 · CP7 slides 21, 25, 35–38, 49–50 (source-marked reference intervals Added)',signal:'4-year-old neutered male outdoor domestic shorthair',history:'Five days of lethargy and poor appetite, fleas present; FeLV/FIV negative; no drugs.',exam:'Pale mildly icteric mucous membranes, HR 200/min, temperature 40.0 °C, soft systolic murmur.',panels:[{name:'Erythrogram and reticulocytes',rows:[['HCT','14 %','30–45'],['RBC','2.4 ×10⁶/µL','6–10'],['MCV','58 fL','39–55'],['Hemoglobin','4.0 g/dL','8–15'],['MCHC','28.6 g/dL','30–36'],['RDW','24 %','17–22'],['Aggregate reticulocytes','6.0 %','0–0.5'],['Absolute aggregate reticulocytes','144 ×10³/µL','<15']]},{name:'Iron, bilirubin and immune testing',rows:[['Serum iron','210 µg/dL','60–170'],['Serum ferritin','Increased','Normal'],['Total bilirubin','2.4 mg/dL','0.1–0.4'],['Direct antiglobulin (Coombs)','Positive','Negative']]}],smear:'Anisocytosis and polychromasia; small coccoid/ring-shaped structures attached to RBC surfaces; no saline-slide autoagglutination.',photos:['Feline M. haemofelis blood film and Coombs example (CP7 slide 38)'],questions:[{q:'Explain the erythrogram: what do high MCV, low MCHC, high RDW and 144 ×10³/µL aggregate reticulocytes indicate?',options:['Strongly regenerative, macrocytic hypochromic anemia','Non-regenerative microcytic anemia','Normal erythrogram','Increased RBC mass from dehydration'],answer:0,why:'Large reticulocytes raise MCV and cell-size heterogeneity; their relative low hemoglobin concentration lowers MCHC. Absolute aggregate reticulocytes 144 ×10³/µL indicate a marked current feline response.'},{q:'What is the main limitation of a positive Coombs test in this cat?',options:['It detects RBC-bound antibody/complement but does not distinguish primary IMHA from infection-associated immune destruction','It proves primary IMHA and rules out hemoplasma','It measures iron stores directly','It detects only Heinz bodies'],answer:0,why:'The positive DAT/Coombs demonstrates immune coating of erythrocytes, which may occur secondary to hemotropic mycoplasmosis. Organisms seen on RBC surfaces and flea exposure make infectious hemolysis a key consideration.'}],differentials:[{name:'Hemotropic mycoplasmosis (M. haemofelis)',tier:'best',support:'RBC-surface organisms, fever, flea exposure, regenerative anemia, icterus and potentially secondary Coombs positivity',against:'Definitive hemoplasma speciation needs PCR',test:'Hemoplasma PCR and careful blood film review'},{name:'Primary immune-mediated hemolytic anemia',tier:'possible',support:'Positive direct antiglobulin test and regenerative hemolysis',against:'RBC-surface organisms provide an infectious trigger; Coombs alone is not specific for primary IMHA',test:'Infectious evaluation before attributing to primary immune disease'},{name:'Heinz-body oxidant hemolysis',tier:'possible',support:'Oxidant exposures can cause hemolytic anemia in cats',against:'No Heinz bodies or oxidant history described',test:'New methylene blue smear and targeted exposure history'},{name:'FeLV-associated anemia',tier:'unlikely',support:'Cats with FeLV may develop anemia',against:'FeLV antigen negative and strong regeneration with surface organisms',test:'Repeat virologic evaluation only if clinical context warrants'},{name:'Chronic blood loss from fleas',tier:'unlikely',support:'Fleas could produce blood loss',against:'Elevated iron/ferritin and icterus favor erythrocyte destruction rather than iron-depleted hemorrhage',test:'Evaluate flea burden and evidence of GI/external bleeding'}]}
,{"id":"8.1","kind":"SBA","title":"Dog · Relative versus absolute reticulocytes","signal":"4-year-old castrated male Beagle","history":"Two weeks of lethargy, tick exposure; no blood loss observed and no drugs.","exam":"Pale gums, HR 140/min, temperature 39.2 °C; no icterus or petechiae.","panels":[{"name":"CBC","rows":[["HCT","15 %","37–55"],["RBC","2.3 ×10⁶/µL","5.5–8.5"],["MCV","65 fL","60–77"],["Hemoglobin","5.0 g/dL","12–18"],["MCHC","33 g/dL","32–36"],["Reticulocytes","3 %","<1"],["Absolute reticulocytes","69 ×10³/µL","~69 in a healthy dog"],["TPP","6.4 g/dL","5.5–7.5"],["Platelets","40 ×10³/µL","200–500"]]}],"smear":"Normocytic/normochromic cells; little polychromasia; morulae-like inclusion in a monocyte; no agglutination.","photos":["Ehrlichia morula in monocyte (CP8 slide 68)","Absolute reticulocyte worked example (CP8 slide 58)"],"questions":[{"q":"Which measurement best judges whether marrow regeneration is adequate?","options":["Absolute reticulocyte count interpreted against severity of anemia","Reticulocyte percentage alone","Normal MCV","Platelet count alone"],"answer":0,"why":"Anemia at HCT 15% with absolute reticulocytes 69 ×10³/µL represents an inadequate response, even though the percentage is 3%."},{"q":"How should this anemia be classified? (Original SBA)","options":["Regenerative because reticulocyte percentage is three times normal","Nonregenerative because absolute reticulocytes match a healthy dog despite HCT 15%","Regenerative because MCV/MCHC are normal","Hemodilution from overhydration","Cannot classify without marrow aspirate"],"answer":1,"why":"3% × RBC 2.3 ×10⁶/µL = 69 ×10³/µL. This is no stronger than a typical healthy absolute reticulocyte count and is inappropriate for two weeks of significant anemia."}],"differentials":[],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 8.1 · CP8 Laboratory Evaluation of Anemia"},{"id":"8.2","kind":"DDx","title":"Dog · Rodenticide hemorrhage and low TPP","signal":"5-year-old castrated male Jack Russell Terrier","history":"Weak and pale for three days. Rat bait box opened in garage five days ago; no IV fluids.","exam":"HR 170/min, weak pulses, pallor, ecchymoses, no icterus or hemoglobinuria.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and plasma","rows":[["HCT","19 %","37–55"],["RBC","2.65 ×10⁶/µL","5.5–8.5"],["MCV","72 fL","60–77"],["Hemoglobin","6.1 g/dL","12–18"],["MCHC","32 g/dL","32–36"],["Reticulocytes","7.5 %","<1"],["Absolute reticulocytes","199 ×10³/µL","<60"],["TPP","3.8 g/dL","5.5–7.5"],["Plasma","Colorless, not hemolyzed","Colorless"],["Platelets","310 ×10³/µL","200–500"]]},{"name":"Coagulation","rows":[["PT","28 s","7–10"]]}],"smear":"Polychromasia, anisocytosis, adequate platelets; no spherocytes or agglutination.","photos":["Regenerative polychromatophilic smear (CP8 slide 66)","HCT/TPP pattern chart (CP8 slide 9)"],"questions":[{"q":"Which pattern links low HCT and low TPP with normal-colored plasma?","options":["Hemorrhage with concurrent protein loss","Extravascular hemolysis alone","Primary polycythemia","Isolated iron overload"],"answer":0,"why":"Whole-blood loss removes erythrocytes and plasma proteins. A colorless plasma sample with a bleeding history supports hemorrhage over pigment-related hemolysis."},{"q":"What does prolonged PT with possible rodenticide exposure prioritize?","options":["Anticoagulant-associated hemorrhage","Heinz body hemolysis","Erythrocytosis","Immune neutropenia"],"answer":0,"why":"PT 28 s (RI 7–10) and bruising with bait exposure suggest anticoagulant coagulopathy. A robust reticulocyte response follows several days after bleeding."}],"differentials":[{"name":"Hemorrhage from anticoagulant rodenticide","tier":"best","support":"Bait history, prolonged PT, ecchymoses, low HCT and TPP","against":"Specific compound and bleeding sites not confirmed","test":"Coagulation testing, directed imaging and exposure review"},{"name":"IMHA","tier":"unlikely","support":"Could cause regeneration","against":"TPP low, plasma colorless, no spherocytes or icterus","test":"Smear/hemolysis testing if indicated"},{"name":"Overhydration / dilution","tier":"unlikely","support":"Can lower HCT and TPP","against":"No administered fluids and reticulocytosis present","test":"Fluid history and follow-up CBC"},{"name":"Chronic intestinal protein/blood loss","tier":"possible","support":"Low TPP and hemorrhage could coexist","against":"MCV normal rather than iron-deficient microcytosis","test":"GI history and fecal testing"},{"name":"Hemangiosarcoma with occult bleeding","tier":"possible","support":"Could cause blood loss and anemia","against":"Documented anticoagulant exposure and PT change more compelling","test":"Abdominal and thoracic imaging"}],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 8.2 · CP8 Laboratory Evaluation of Anemia"},{"id":"8.3","kind":"SBA","title":"Horse · Marrow evidence of regeneration","signal":"7-year-old Thoroughbred gelding","history":"Lethargy and dark urine five days after a storm brought wilted red maple leaves into the paddock.","exam":"HR 70/min, RR 30/min, icteric gums, brown urine, temperature 38.6 °C.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and marrow","rows":[["HCT","16 %","32–52"],["RBC","3.0 ×10⁶/µL","6.5–12.5"],["MCV","53 fL","37–49"],["Hemoglobin","5.6 g/dL","11–19"],["MCHC","35 g/dL","31–37"],["RDW","22 %","17–20"],["Peripheral reticulocytes","None seen","None"],["TPP","6.9 g/dL","5.8–8.0"],["Plasma","Icteric","Colorless to pale yellow"],["Marrow M:E","0.16","~1"]]}],"smear":"Anisocytosis, Heinz bodies and eccentrocytes; no peripheral polychromasia. Bone marrow shows erythroid hyperplasia and reticulocytes.","photos":["Equine marrow erythroid hyperplasia, M:E 0.16 (CP8 slide 20)"],"questions":[{"q":"What does low M:E ratio with anemia and increased MCV/RDW suggest?","options":["Marrow erythroid hyperplasia","Marrow aplasia","Normal species-typical marrow","Iron deficiency from chronic loss"],"answer":0,"why":"Horses do not ordinarily release reticulocytes into peripheral blood; marrow erythroid hyperplasia with low M:E and rising MCV/RDW reveals regeneration."},{"q":"No blood reticulocytes are seen. What is the best interpretation? (Original SBA)","options":["Nonregenerative because blood retics are absent","Regenerative based on marrow hyperplasia, M:E 0.16, and increased MCV/RDW","Pregnancy hemodilution","Anemia of inflammatory disease","Iron deficiency"],"answer":1,"why":"Equine regeneration is evaluated via marrow and changes in indices, not circulating reticulocytes. Oxidant damage and icteric plasma point to increased destruction."}],"differentials":[],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 8.3 · CP8 Laboratory Evaluation of Anemia"},{"id":"8.4","kind":"DDx","title":"Dog · Physiologic gestational hemodilution","signal":"3-year-old intact female Labrador, day 56 pregnancy","history":"Eating normally at prenatal check; no drugs, observed bleeding or gastrointestinal signs.","exam":"Bright with pink mucous membranes, HR 100/min, palpable fetuses, no murmur or icterus.","panels":[{"name":"Erythrogram and protein","rows":[["HCT","33 %","37–55"],["RBC","4.9 ×10⁶/µL","5.5–8.5"],["MCV","67 fL","60–77"],["Hemoglobin","11.2 g/dL","12–18"],["MCHC","34 g/dL","32–36"],["Reticulocytes","0.4 %","<1"],["Absolute reticulocytes","20 ×10³/µL","<60"],["TPP","5.6 g/dL","5.5–7.5"],["Albumin","2.5 g/dL","2.6–3.6"],["Plasma","Colorless","Colorless"]]}],"smear":"Normal erythrocyte morphology without polychromasia or agglutination; platelets normal.","photos":["Canine gestation and lactation HCT curve (CP8 slide 11)"],"questions":[{"q":"What pattern explains mild low HCT with no reticulocytosis in an otherwise healthy late-pregnant dog?","options":["Gestational expansion of plasma volume","Strong hemolysis","Iron deficiency with fragmented cells","Hemorrhage with prolonged PT"],"answer":0,"why":"The course describes pregnancy-related plasma-volume expansion as a physiologic reduction in measured hematocrit without requiring an increased reticulocyte count."},{"q":"What does near-normal TPP and mildly low albumin add?","options":["Compatible with dilution in an otherwise well patient","Proves blood loss","Confirms marrow neoplasia","Shows isolated hyperglobulinemia"],"answer":0,"why":"Dilution can produce mild lower HCT/protein values without abnormal morphology or clinical illness; trend after lactation."}],"differentials":[{"name":"Hemodilution of late gestation","tier":"best","support":"Well late-pregnant dog, mild normocytic anemia, no reticulocytosis, slightly low albumin","against":"Should be rechecked for resolution","test":"Repeat CBC after lactation/weaning"},{"name":"Occult hemorrhage","tier":"possible","support":"May reduce HCT","against":"No bleeding history, marked TPP decrease or expected regeneration","test":"Serial CBC, stool/urine and blood loss review"},{"name":"Chronic nonregenerative anemia","tier":"possible","support":"Would fit indices and reticulocytes","against":"Pregnancy explains mild changes more parsimoniously","test":"Recheck post-pregnancy; broaden work-up if persistent"},{"name":"Iatrogenic overhydration","tier":"unlikely","support":"Dilution can mimic anemia","against":"No IV fluid history","test":"Review fluid exposure"},{"name":"Splenic sequestration","tier":"unlikely","support":"Can transiently lower HCT","against":"No anesthesia, sedation or splenomegaly","test":"Clinical/history review"}],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 8.4 · CP8 Laboratory Evaluation of Anemia"},{"id":"8.5","kind":"SBA","title":"Cat · Punctate reticulocytes reveal recovering anemia","signal":"3-year-old neutered male domestic shorthair","history":"Hit by car 12 days ago with hemoabdomen (initial HCT 11%); splenectomy without transfusion. Now bright and eating.","exam":"Pink gums, HR 180/min, healed surgical wound, no icterus.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and feline reticulocytes","rows":[["HCT","23 %","30–45"],["RBC","3.3 ×10⁶/µL","6–10"],["MCV","70 fL","39–55"],["MCHC","32 g/dL","30–36"],["Aggregate reticulocytes","0.2 %","0–0.5"],["Punctate reticulocytes","83 %","1–10"],["Absolute aggregate reticulocytes","7 ×10³/µL","<15"],["Absolute punctate reticulocytes","~2,700 ×10³/µL","~140"],["TPP","7.2 g/dL","6.0–8.0"]]}],"smear":"Macrocytes and mild anisocytosis, few polychromatophilic cells, no parasites or Howell–Jolly bodies; many punctate cells on NMB stain.","photos":["Feline punctate reticulocytes on Wright–Giemsa and reticulocyte stain (CP8 slide 54)"],"questions":[{"q":"Why do punctate reticulocytes remain abundant after aggregate reticulocytes decline?","options":["Punctate cells persist for a week or more after the earlier response","Punctate cells prove current marrow aplasia","They are all hemotropic organisms","They occur only in kittens"],"answer":0,"why":"Feline aggregate reticulocytes mature rapidly into punctate forms, which retain RNA dots for longer, preserving evidence of a prior regenerative response."},{"q":"What does punctate 83% with aggregate 0.2% indicate? (Original SBA)","options":["Nonregenerative anemia without previous response","Strong earlier regeneration now resolving","Spurious parasite-related reticulocytosis","Overhydration","Anemia of the neonate"],"answer":1,"why":"HCT improved from 11% to 23%; high MCV and abundant punctate reticulocytes signal recovering regenerative anemia even though aggregate reticulocytes are now low."}],"differentials":[],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 8.5 · CP8 Laboratory Evaluation of Anemia"},{"id":"9.1","kind":"DDx","title":"Dog · Autoagglutination and spherocytic hemolysis","signal":"6-year-old spayed female Cocker Spaniel","history":"Four days of weakness and lethargy, dark orange (not red) urine; no known drug, toxin or tick exposure.","exam":"HR 168/min, pallor, icterus, fever 39.8 °C and mild splenomegaly.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and inflammatory markers","rows":[["HCT","14 %","37–54"],["RBC","1.75 ×10⁶/µL","5.5–8.5"],["Hemoglobin","5.3 g/dL","12–18"],["MCV","80 fL","62–74"],["MCHC","38 g/dL","32–36"],["Absolute reticulocytes","310 ×10³/µL","<80"],["TPP","6.9 g/dL","6.0–7.8"],["Platelets","210 ×10³/µL","160–430"],["WBC","32.0 ×10³/µL","6–17"],["Neutrophils","26.4 ×10³/µL","3.0–11.5"],["Bands","1.6 ×10³/µL","0–0.3"],["Total bilirubin","4.1 mg/dL","0.1–0.4"]]}],"smear":"Strong autoagglutination and >5 spherocytes per 100× field, polychromasia, nucleated RBCs, some ghost cells. Plasma yellow, urine bilirubin positive; no red supernatant.","photos":["Autoagglutination and spherocytes (CP9 slides 30–31, 35–37)"],"questions":[{"q":"What RBC evidence most strongly supports immune-mediated hemolysis?","options":["Autoagglutination with many spherocytes","A normal platelet count alone","Low total protein alone","Absent reticulocytes"],"answer":0,"why":"Autoagglutination and numerous spherocytes are strong signs of immune-related erythrocyte destruction; absolute reticulocytes 310 support regeneration."},{"q":"Why is calculated MCHC 38 g/dL unreliable?","options":["Agglutination can artifactually elevate MCHC","True RBCs synthesize unlimited hemoglobin","Iron depletion always raises MCHC","High MCHC proves marrow aplasia"],"answer":0,"why":"Agglutinated cells distort analyzer RBC count and indices, making calculated MCHC spuriously high."}],"differentials":[{"name":"Primary IMHA","tier":"best","support":"HCT 14, marked spherocytes and agglutination, regeneration and jaundice","against":"Secondary triggers still need exclusion","test":"Saline dispersion, directed infectious/drug and immune evaluation"},{"name":"Secondary IMHA (infectious/drug/transfusion)","tier":"possible","support":"Can produce same RBC morphology","against":"No trigger established in history","test":"Babesia PCR, exposure and transfusion review"},{"name":"Systemic lupus erythematosus","tier":"possible","support":"Can have immune-mediated erythrocyte destruction","against":"No extra-organ immune features described","test":"ANA and renal/joint assessment if warranted"},{"name":"Oxidant hemolysis","tier":"unlikely","support":"Can cause hemolysis and icterus","against":"No Heinz bodies, strong spherocytes and agglutination","test":"NMB stain and toxin history"},{"name":"Fragmentation anemia / DIC","tier":"unlikely","support":"Can cause anemia","against":"No schistocytes or thrombocytopenia","test":"Coagulation testing if suspicion develops"},{"name":"External blood loss","tier":"unlikely","support":"Can cause regeneration","against":"TPP normal, icterus and hemolytic morphology","test":"Targeted bleeding assessment"}],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 9.1 · CP9 Regenerative Anemia"},{"id":"9.2","kind":"SBA","title":"Cat · Hemoplasma with secondary immune destruction","signal":"2-year-old neutered male outdoor domestic shorthair","history":"Five days of anorexia and lethargy with fleas; no drug history.","exam":"HR 220/min, pale and icteric gums, fever 40.0 °C, weakness.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and immune testing","rows":[["HCT","11 %","30–45"],["RBC","2.1 ×10⁶/µL","6–10"],["Hemoglobin","3.2 g/dL","9–15"],["MCV","52 fL","39–50"],["MCHC","29 g/dL","30–36"],["Aggregate reticulocytes","105 ×10³/µL","<15"],["TPP","7.8 g/dL","6.0–8.0"],["Total bilirubin","2.1 mg/dL","0.1–0.4"],["Direct Coombs","Positive","Negative"]]}],"smear":"Polychromasia with small coccoid/ring-shaped structures on RBC surfaces; a few spherocytes.","photos":["Feline M. haemofelis organisms on RBC surfaces (CP9 slides 45–46)"],"questions":[{"q":"Why doesn't a positive Coombs test establish primary IMHA?","options":["An infection such as hemoplasma may trigger secondary antibody coating","Positive Coombs detects only iron deficiency","Coombs is a feline platelet test","It excludes concurrent infection"],"answer":0,"why":"RBC-bound antibody may be secondary to hemotropic infection; visible RBC-surface organisms and exposure history direct the diagnostic approach."},{"q":"Which is the best interpretation of this cat's anemia? (Original SBA)","options":["Primary IMHA proved by Coombs","Cytauxzoon felis with marked regeneration","Mycoplasma haemofelis with secondary immune-mediated extravascular hemolysis","Heinz-body oxidant hemolysis","Chronic flea blood loss with iron deficiency"],"answer":2,"why":"Organisms on RBC surfaces plus macrocytic regenerative anemia and a positive Coombs test support feline hemoplasmosis with a secondary immune component, not proof of primary IMHA."}],"differentials":[],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 9.2 · CP9 Regenerative Anemia"},{"id":"9.3","kind":"DDx","title":"Horse · Red maple oxidant hemolysis and pigmenturia","signal":"9-year-old gelding","history":"Depression two days after a storm and wilted maple branches in the paddock; no new drugs.","exam":"HR 72/min, RR 40/min, brown-tinged icteric gums, red-brown urine, temperature 38.6 °C.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and pigment changes","rows":[["HCT","14 %","32–48"],["RBC","3.5 ×10⁶/µL","6.5–12.5"],["Hemoglobin","5.0 g/dL","11–19"],["MCV","40 fL","34–58"],["MCHC","36 g/dL","31–37"],["Peripheral reticulocytes","0","None normally"],["TPP","7.1 g/dL","5.8–8.7"],["Total bilirubin","6.5 mg/dL","1.0–2.0"]]},{"name":"Inflammation and renal","rows":[["WBC","14.8 ×10³/µL","5.5–12.5"],["Neutrophils","10.9 ×10³/µL","2.7–6.7"],["BUN","42 mg/dL","11–27"],["Creatinine","3.1 mg/dL","1.0–1.8"],["Urine heme","Strong positive; no sediment RBCs; red supernatant","Negative"]]}],"smear":"Heinz bodies on NMB stain and eccentrocytes, no spherocytes/polychromasia. Marrow not yet sampled.","photos":["Equine marrow erythroid hyperplasia (CP9 slide 10)","Heinz body smear (source notes no equine slide; use approved asset later)"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the strongest evidence of intravascular hemolysis?","options":["Red urine supernatant with positive heme and absent sediment erythrocytes","Icterus alone","High bilirubin alone","No circulating reticulocytes"],"answer":0,"why":"The source emphasizes hemoglobinemia/hemoglobinuria to identify intravascular hemolysis; bilirubinemia and icterus alone do not distinguish intravascular from extravascular processes."},{"q":"What smear findings point to an oxidant mechanism?","options":["Heinz bodies and eccentrocytes","Isolated spherocytes","Normal RBCs","Basophilic stippling alone"],"answer":0,"why":"Heinz bodies indicate denatured hemoglobin and eccentrocytes indicate oxidized membrane damage, compatible with red maple exposure."}],"differentials":[{"name":"Red maple oxidant toxicosis","tier":"best","support":"Wilted maple exposure, Heinz bodies, brown mucosa and hemoglobinuria","against":"Leaf identity unconfirmed","test":"Identify foliage, NMB stain and methemoglobin screen"},{"name":"Other oxidants (phenothiazine)","tier":"possible","support":"Similar oxidative RBC lesions","against":"No new drug reported","test":"Medication/feed history"},{"name":"IMHA","tier":"unlikely","support":"Can cause hemolysis","against":"No spherocytes or autoagglutination, oxidant changes present","test":"Immune hemolysis tests if necessary"},{"name":"Equine infectious anemia","tier":"possible","support":"Can cause anemia and fever","against":"Oxidant morphology is more specific","test":"Coggins AGID/ELISA"},{"name":"Leptospira/Clostridium infection","tier":"possible","support":"Potential hemolysis with systemic organ abnormalities","against":"No confirmed pathogen","test":"Targeted infectious testing"},{"name":"Liver failure","tier":"unlikely","support":"Could cause icterus","against":"Does not explain Heinz body hemoglobinuria","test":"Hepatic chemistry and function"},{"name":"Hereditary RBC antioxidant defect","tier":"unlikely","support":"Can predispose to oxidant damage","against":"Adult onset with clear environmental trigger","test":"RBC enzyme testing if recurrent"}],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 9.3 · CP9 Regenerative Anemia"},{"id":"9.4","kind":"SBA","title":"Cow · Postpartum hypophosphatemic hemolysis","signal":"5-year-old Holstein cow, four days postpartum","history":"Off feed and lower milk output, red urine today; fed high-concentrate diet without brassica access or tick-endemic exposure.","exam":"HR 96/min, pallor, mild icterus, temperature 38.8 °C, no ticks.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and chemistry","rows":[["HCT","15 %","24–46"],["RBC","3.0 ×10⁶/µL","5.0–10.0"],["Hemoglobin","5.2 g/dL","8–15"],["MCV","50 fL","40–60"],["MCHC","35 g/dL","30–36"],["TPP","7.0 g/dL","6.0–8.0"],["Phosphorus","1.1 mg/dL","4.5–8.0"],["Calcium","8.6 mg/dL","8.0–10.4"],["Total bilirubin","1.2 mg/dL","0–0.4"]]}],"smear":"Mild polychromasia, basophilic stippling and anisocytosis; no RBC organisms or Heinz bodies. Urine supernatant remains red after centrifuging.","photos":["Bovine polychromasia and basophilic stippling (CP9 slides 8–9)"],"questions":[{"q":"What does red urine supernatant without sediment RBCs indicate?","options":["Hemoglobinuria consistent with intravascular hemolysis","Only lower urinary tract bleeding","Normal postpartum urine","Only bilirubinuria"],"answer":0,"why":"Heme-positive urine without sediment RBCs suggests pigmenturia. Combined severe anemia and postpartum hypophosphatemia support intravascular red-cell damage."},{"q":"Which is the most likely cause of hemolysis in this cow? (Original SBA)","options":["Anaplasma marginale","Brassica oxidant toxicity","Severe hypophosphatemia reducing RBC ATP","Hypotonic fluid intoxication","Hereditary spherocytosis"],"answer":2,"why":"Phosphorus 1.1 mg/dL and postpartum hemoglobinuria favor phosphate-dependent energy failure; no Heinz bodies or organisms support the competing causes."}],"differentials":[],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 9.4 · CP9 Regenerative Anemia"},{"id":"9.5","kind":"DDx","title":"Dog · Hemoabdomen and fragmentation with DIC","signal":"10-year-old castrated male German Shepherd","history":"Four days of lethargy followed by collapse; no known toxin/drug exposure.","exam":"HR 150/min, pale gums, weak pulses, abdominal fluid wave; nonclotting bloody fluid on abdominocentesis.","panels":[{"name":"CBC and proteins","rows":[["HCT","21 %","37–54"],["RBC","3.0 ×10⁶/µL","5.5–8.5"],["Hemoglobin","7.3 g/dL","12–18"],["MCV","70 fL","62–74"],["MCHC","35 g/dL","32–36"],["Absolute reticulocytes","160 ×10³/µL","<80"],["TPP","4.4 g/dL","6.0–7.8"],["Platelets","38 ×10³/µL","160–430"],["WBC","21.4 ×10³/µL","6–17"],["Neutrophils","17.9 ×10³/µL","3.0–11.5"],["Bands","0.4 ×10³/µL","0–0.3"]]},{"name":"Coagulation and pigment","rows":[["PT","14.8 s","7.0–10.0"],["aPTT","24 s","11–17"],["Fibrinogen","90 mg/dL","100–400"],["Icterus index","<5","<5"]]}],"smear":"Schistocytes, acanthocytes, nucleated RBCs, polychromasia; scant platelets and macroplatelets.","photos":["Schistocytes, acanthocytes, metarubricytes (CP9 slide 66)","DIC smear and macroplatelets (CP9 slide 65)"],"questions":[{"q":"Which evidence supports fragmentation with consumption?","options":["Schistocytes plus low platelets, prolonged PT/aPTT and low fibrinogen","Only increased reticulocyte percentage","Icterus without red cells","Isolated eosinophilia"],"answer":0,"why":"The fragmentation morphology and broad hemostatic abnormalities indicate a microangiopathic process and consumptive coagulopathy."},{"q":"What does low HCT with low TPP and nonclotting hemoabdomen suggest?","options":["Internal hemorrhage contributing to the anemia","Pure extravascular hemolysis only","Absolute erythrocytosis","No blood loss"],"answer":0,"why":"Bleeding into a body cavity reduces circulating erythrocytes and proteins; later reticulocytosis reflects the response over days."}],"differentials":[{"name":"Hemangiosarcoma with secondary DIC","tier":"best","support":"Hemoabdomen, schistocytes, thrombocytopenia and prolonged coagulation times","against":"Mass not yet confirmed","test":"Abdominal imaging, thoracic assessment and definitive pathology if indicated"},{"name":"DIC from another systemic disease","tier":"possible","support":"Same consumptive laboratory pattern","against":"No alternate primary illness demonstrated","test":"Inflammation/sepsis and neoplasia investigation"},{"name":"Heartworm caval syndrome","tier":"possible","support":"Can fragment erythrocytes","against":"No described hemoglobinuria or heart findings","test":"Heartworm test and echocardiography"},{"name":"Vasculitis / microangiopathy","tier":"possible","support":"Can cause fragments","against":"Less likely to explain hemoabdomen","test":"Chemistry/urinalysis"},{"name":"Anticoagulant rodenticide","tier":"unlikely","support":"Hemorrhage and prolonged clotting times possible","against":"Normal platelets and no schistocytes would be expected","test":"Exposure history/PIVKA"},{"name":"IMHA","tier":"unlikely","support":"Anemia can occur","against":"No spherocytes/agglutination and absent icterus","test":"Review smear and targeted hemolysis tests"}],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 9.5 · CP9 Regenerative Anemia"},{"id":"9.6","kind":"SBA","title":"Kitten · Neonatal isoerythrolysis after nursing","signal":"4-day-old domestic shorthair kitten; type A, born to type B queen and type A tom","history":"Nursed normally. Two littermates died; kittens became weak and icteric on day three.","exam":"Weak, pale and icteric, dark red-brown urine, tail-tip discoloration, temperature 36.8 °C.","panels":[{"name":"Neonatal CBC","rows":[["HCT","14 %","35–50"],["RBC","3.2 ×10⁶/µL","6.0–10.0"],["Hemoglobin","4.5 g/dL","10–16"],["MCV","44 fL","39–55"],["MCHC","32 g/dL","30–36"],["Aggregate reticulocytes","70 ×10³/µL","<15"],["Total bilirubin","3.4 mg/dL","0.1–0.4"],["TPP","5.2 g/dL","4.5–6.5"]]}],"smear":"Polychromasia, anisocytosis and some autoagglutination; urine supernatant red (hemoglobinuria).","photos":["Feline blood typing card and kitten tail-tip lesion (not provided; lecture slide 43 is text only)"],"questions":[{"q":"Where do incompatible antibodies come from in feline neonatal isoerythrolysis?","options":["Naturally occurring maternal anti-A antibodies absorbed in colostrum","Kitten's own mature immune response","Only prenatal placental transfer","The sire's circulating serum after birth"],"answer":0,"why":"Type B queens may carry naturally occurring strong anti-A antibodies. Type A kittens acquire them by drinking colostrum after birth."},{"q":"Which statement best explains this kitten's disease? (Original SBA)","options":["Maternal antibodies crossed the placenta and caused anemia at birth","Type B queen's natural anti-A antibodies were absorbed from colostrum after nursing","Type A queen bred to type B tom causes identical risk","Queen must first be sensitized by earlier litter as in mares","Kitten synthesized its own anti-A antibodies"],"answer":1,"why":"The source identifies type B queen × type A offspring with postnursing hemolysis as feline neonatal isoerythrolysis. Unlike mares, type B queens can already have clinically significant natural anti-A antibodies."}],"differentials":[],"source":"Cases_Part1.docx · Case 9.6 · CP9 Regenerative Anemia"}
